==English==
{{wikipedia|habanera (music)}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|es-CU|habanera}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A style of music from [[Cuba]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Brian Seibert|title=Review: Tapping Out Some New World Rhythms|work=The New York Times|date=2018-03-05|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/03/05/arts/dance/review-rags-strides-and-habaneras-jazz-at-lincoln-center.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=The programming was far from random. The '''habanera''' is a hybrid seed of great potency. An adaptation of the European contradanza by slaves from West Africa, it is not only an early version of a New World rhythm that took over the globe, but also a direct ancestor for much of jazz and rock ‘n’ roll.}}
# A dance performed to this music.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Edward Rothstein|title=Music: 40 Songs of Cuba|work=The New York Times|date=1982-12-27|page=C12|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1982/12/27/arts/music-40-songs-of-cuba.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=The music of Cuba has had a worldwide influence on forms of song and dance. René Buch who conceived and directed a musical “collage” called “¡Habana!” at the Repertorio Español on East 27th Street, points out the influence on Spain, for example, which received ‘'''habaneras'''’ and ‘contradanzas’ from Cuba; in this century, in the 1920's, the “rumba” and such songs as “El Manisero” and “Siboney” made their way north to become part of United States culture.}}

